%section.text-center.py-5
  %h1.display-4.fw-bold.mb-3= site-title
  %p.lead.text-body-secondary.mb-4.mx-auto{style: 'max-width: 40rem'} An insanely fast static site generator written in C++. Point it at a directory of Markdown and it writes a directory of HTML you can host anywhere. No database, no runtime, nothing to install alongside it.
  .d-flex.gap-2.justify-content-center.flex-wrap
    %a.btn.btn-primary.btn-lg{href: '/guide/'} Read the guide
    %a.btn.btn-secondary.btn-lg{href: '/cli/'} CLI
    %a.btn.btn-secondary.btn-lg{href: '/reference/'} Reference

%section.py-4
  .row.row-cols-1.row-cols-md-2.row-cols-lg-3.g-4
    .col
      %a.card.h-100.feature-tile.text-decoration-none.text-reset{href: '/guide/writing-posts/'}
        .card-body
          %h5.card-title Markdown and HAML
          %p.card-text.text-body-secondary Write posts in Markdown with front matter. Shape pages with HAML layouts and reusable partials.
    .col
      %a.card.h-100.feature-tile.text-decoration-none.text-reset{href: '/guide/layouts/'}
        .card-body
          %h5.card-title Sections and navigation
          %p.card-text.text-body-secondary Sections come from the content tree, with clean URLs, a recursive menu, paginated listings, and tag pages.
    .col
      %a.card.h-100.feature-tile.text-decoration-none.text-reset{href: '/guide/feeds-and-search/'}
        .card-body
          %h5.card-title Feeds and search
          %p.card-text.text-body-secondary Atom feeds, a sitemap, and browser search against a prebuilt index, all generated at build time.
    .col
      %a.card.h-100.feature-tile.text-decoration-none.text-reset{href: '/guide/css-frameworks/'}
        .card-body
          %h5.card-title CSS frameworks
          %p.card-text.text-body-secondary Framework profiles for Bootstrap 5, Pico, and Bulma add classes to the same semantic HTML, or use none.
    .col
      %a.card.h-100.feature-tile.text-decoration-none.text-reset{href: '/guide/syntax-highlighting/'}
        .card-body
          %h5.card-title Syntax highlighting
          %p.card-text.text-body-secondary Fenced code blocks are highlighted server-side during the build, so pages need no client-side highlighter.
    .col
      %a.card.h-100.feature-tile.text-decoration-none.text-reset{href: '/guide/previewing-and-building/'}
        .card-body
          %h5.card-title Preview and incremental builds
          %p.card-text.text-body-secondary A preview server rebuilds on change and live-reloads the open page. A rebuild does only the work the change requires, and lands byte-identical to a build from scratch.
    .col
      %a.card.h-100.feature-tile.text-decoration-none.text-reset{href: '/guide/previewing-and-building/#asset-optimization'}
        .card-body
          %h5.card-title Asset pipeline
          %p.card-text.text-body-secondary Stylesheets and scripts are minified, every asset can carry a hash of its content so a far-future cache never serves a stale file, and raster images get a srcset of smaller widths.
    .col
      %a.card.h-100.feature-tile.text-decoration-none.text-reset{href: '/guide/internationalization/'}
        .card-body
          %h5.card-title Write in more than one language
          %p.card-text.text-body-secondary Each language builds into its own subtree with its own listings, taxonomies, and feeds. Translations are matched by filename, so a layout can offer a switcher that lands on the right page.
    .col
      %a.card.h-100.feature-tile.text-decoration-none.text-reset{href: '/guide/getting-started/'}
        .card-body
          %h5.card-title No dependencies
          %p.card-text.text-body-secondary Markdown, HAML, JSON, YAML, the asset pipeline, and the preview server are all in the binary. Building it needs a compiler and CMake, and nothing else.
